            In the Regime Forfettario, INPS is calculated only on the coefficient of profitability (78% for software and marketing). You pay 26.07% on €46,800 instead of the full €60,000 turnover.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        2. Spain Beckham Law Rules for Autónomos & Remote Contractors
      </h2>
      <p>
        Under Spain's Startup Act (<em>Ley de Startups</em>), foreign remote workers entering on the Spain Digital Nomad Visa can access the <strong>Beckham Law (Régimen Especial de Trabajadores Desplazados - RETD)</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Flat 24% IRPF:</strong> Applies to all worldwide employment and freelance income up to <strong>€600,000</strong> for up to 6 consecutive tax years (earnings above €600,000 are taxed at 47%).</li>
        <li><strong>Autónomo Social Security Quota:</strong> Under Spain's contribution system based on real net earnings (<em>cotización por ingresos reales</em>), freelancers pay between €320 and €542 per month in social security.</li>
        <li><strong>Certificate of Coverage Exemption:</strong> If you are employed by a foreign company or can utilize a bilateral Social Security totalization treaty (e.g. US, UK, Canada), you can present a Certificate of Coverage and remain exempt from Spanish social security entirely for up to 3 to 5 years, boosting net savings by €4,000–€6,500 annually.</li>
        <li><strong>Strict 6-Month Filing Deadline:</strong> You must file <strong>Modelo 149</strong> with the Agencia Tributaria within 6 months of your social security registration, otherwise you default to standard progressive rates up to 47%.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        3. Strategic Summary: The Relocation Decision Matrix
      </h2>
      <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-emerald-500/30 my-6">
        <h3 class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-base mb-3">Which Visa & Tax Strategy Should You File?</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs text-slate-300">
          <p>
            • <strong>If your gross annual freelance billing is between €35,000 and €85,000:</strong> Choose <strong>Italy (Regime Forfettario)</strong>. The 5% substitute tax combined with the 78% profitability coefficient yields an effective tax rate of just 23.2% including all pension contributions. It outperforms both Spain's Beckham Law and Portugal's NHR.
          </p>
          <p>
            • <strong>If your gross annual revenue exceeds €100,000:</strong> Choose <strong>Spain (Beckham Law)</strong>. Italy's strict €85,000 Forfettario ceiling disqualifies you, forcing you into ordinary progressive IRPEF or the revised Impatriati regime where INPS and progressive rates eat into your income. Spain's predictable 24% flat rate guarantees higher liquidity and simplicity.
          </p>
          <p>
            • <strong>If you have dependents or relocate to Southern Italy:</strong> Italy's <strong>Lavoratori Impatriati</strong> regime can grant a 60% to 70% exemption, which at high income levels (above €150k where INPS contributions are fully capped at €31k) creates a competitive net take-home of over €108k annually.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

